**Cross-cutting via pipeline behaviors:**

```csharp
public class ValidationBehavior<TReq, TRes> : IPipelineBehavior<TReq, TRes>
{
    public async Task<TRes> Handle(TReq r, RequestHandlerDelegate<TRes> next, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await _validator.ValidateAndThrowAsync(r, ct);
        return await next();
    }
}
```

Register behaviors in order: logging → validation → transaction → handler.

```csharp
public async IAsyncEnumerable<Order> ReadOrdersAsync(
    [EnumeratorCancellation] CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    string? cursor = null;
    do
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        var page = await _http.GetFromJsonAsync<Page<Order>>(
            $"/orders?cursor={cursor}", ct)
            ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("null page");

        foreach (var o in page.Items)
            yield return o;

        cursor = page.NextCursor;
    } while (cursor is not null);
}

// consumer
await foreach (var order in repo.ReadOrdersAsync(ct).WithCancellation(ct))
{
    await ProcessAsync(order, ct);
}
```

**Why this works:**
- `[EnumeratorCancellation]` flows the consumer's token into the iterator.
- `await foreach` pulls one page at a time — bounded memory.
- Backpressure is natural: the producer waits for the consumer.

**Pitfalls:** wrap network calls with retry/Polly; don't materialize with `.ToListAsync()` on huge streams; close resources via `await using`.

```csharp
var channel = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
{
    FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait,
    SingleReader = false,
    SingleWriter = true
});

// producer
_ = Task.Run(async () =>
{
    foreach (var item in source)
        await channel.Writer.WriteAsync(item, ct);
    channel.Writer.Complete();
});

// N consumers
var consumers = Enumerable.Range(0, 8).Select(_ => Task.Run(async () =>
{
    await foreach (var item in channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        await HandleAsync(item, ct);
}));

await Task.WhenAll(consumers);
```

**Why Channels over BlockingCollection / ConcurrentQueue:**
- Async-first (no blocking threads)
- Built-in backpressure via `BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait`
- Clean completion signaling via `Writer.Complete()`

Use for in-process pipelines; use Service Bus / Event Hubs for cross-process.
